WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:02.996
(gentle music)

00:00:02.996 --> 00:00:06.496
- [Woman] This is Aspen, a two-toed sloth.

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:13.653
Sloths spend almost their
entire lives hanging in trees

00:00:13.653 --> 00:00:16.392
and do nearly everything upside down.

00:00:16.392 --> 00:00:20.059
Eating, sleeping, mating,
even giving birth.

00:00:21.778 --> 00:00:24.494
Since it spends so much time upside down,

00:00:24.494 --> 00:00:26.964
a sloth's fur grows in
the opposite direction

00:00:26.964 --> 00:00:28.671
of all other mammals.

00:00:28.671 --> 00:00:32.383
This allows water to run
off the sloth when it rains.

00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:36.600
Its fur also has minute grooves
that collect and grow algae,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.971
giving wild sloths a greenish tint

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:41.888
and providing excellent camouflage.

00:00:42.987 --> 00:00:45.765
A sloth's powerful claws
are perfectly adapted

00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:47.583
for gripping and climbing trees,

00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:50.574
but become a nuisance when on the ground.

00:00:50.574 --> 00:00:53.559
Surprisingly though they
are excellent swimmers

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:55.597
and have been known to drop into the water

00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:57.514
and swim to a new tree.

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:03.284
Despite these adaptations,
sloths face many threats.

00:01:03.284 --> 00:01:06.660
Their forest homes are
increasingly becoming fragmented,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.750
making breeding difficult,

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.423
and they are a popular
target for traffickers

00:01:11.423 --> 00:01:13.860
in the illegal pet trade.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.110
This is the two-toed sloth.

